{
  "global_card_id": "drawdown",
  "global_canonical": "Drawdown",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "Risk Management",
  "aliases": [
    "Drawdown Limit",
    "Max Drawdown"
  ],
  "definition": "Drawdown is the decline in a trading account from its peak equity to a subsequent trough, measuring the loss endured before recovery. In prop firm contexts it also denotes the maximum loss allowance before an account fails—and that drawdown amount, not the nominal account size, represents the true risk capital that should drive position sizing.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "Education - ICT (Updated)": "The maximum loss allowance on a prop firm account before it is failed. On futures prop firm accounts the drawdown amount—not the nominal account size—represents the true risk capital available and should be the basis for all position sizing calculations.",
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement": "The decline from the peak value of a trading account to its lowest point during a specific period, measuring the loss experienced before recovery, typically expressed as a percentage drop from peak to trough."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "Identify the highest account equity point before a decline as the peak, and the lowest subsequent equity point as the trough (SMC-Strong-Weak emphasis).",
    "Calculate drawdown as the percentage drop from peak to trough.",
    "In prop firm contexts, identify the firm's stated maximum loss limit (e.g., $1,500 on a 25K account) as the drawdown figure (ICT emphasis).",
    "Treat the drawdown amount, not the nominal account size, as the actual capital at risk.",
    "Calculate risk per trade as a percentage of the drawdown amount rather than of the nominal account size.",
    "Target risk per trade of 7–10% of the drawdown, allowing 10–15 consecutive losing trades before the account is blown (ICT)."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Sizing position as a percentage of the nominal account size (e.g., 25K) instead of the drawdown amount, leading to oversized risk (ICT).",
    "Risking 1% of the nominal account when it actually equates to ~17% of the drawdown, blowing the account after only 6–7 losing trades (ICT).",
    "Failing to lower risk after passing the evaluation phase, missing the chance to build a cushion on the funded account (ICT).",
    "Assuming small percentage losses are easily recovered without recognizing the exponential difficulty of recovery (SMC-Strong-Weak).",
    "Trading more aggressively during drawdowns to recoup losses quickly, worsening the situation.",
    "Neglecting to reduce position size or risk during high-volatility periods, leading to larger drawdowns."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Drawdown is central to capital preservation, but the two sources stress different applications. SMC-Strong-Weak focuses on recovery math: large drawdowns demand disproportionately larger gains to recover (a 50% loss needs a 100% gain), so traders should stay defensive and cut risk during losing or high-volatility periods. ICT focuses on prop firm sizing: the drawdown allowance—not nominal account size—is true risk capital, so risk should be set at 7–10% of the drawdown to survive 10–15 consecutive losses during evaluation. Risking 1% of a 25K account when drawdown is only $1,500 equals ~17% of true risk and blows the account in 6–7 losses. Once funded, ICT lowers risk to build a cushion; the same logic applies to Forex prop firms with different numbers.",
  "merge_notes": "The two sources define the same concept from different angles. SMC-Strong-Weak treats drawdown as a descriptive equity-curve metric (peak-to-trough decline) centered on recovery difficulty. ICT treats it as a prop firm loss-limit constraint that should govern position sizing. The percentage figures, account examples (25K / $1,500), and 7–10% / 10–15-loss targets are specific to ICT and do not appear in SMC-Strong-Weak.",
